@ lang.5.narratology
~ Whose account? The Open Window
from: https://en.wikisource.org/w/index.php?title=Beasts_and_Super-Beasts/The_Open_Window&oldid=10921512
goal: Use Saki’s original story to separate a character’s claims from the narrator’s reports, weigh later evidence, and distinguish narrative voice from access to a character’s thoughts.
intro: In Saki’s “The Open Window”, Framton Nuttel visits Mrs. Sappleton and meets her niece Vera. A narrator can report what a character says without confirming that the character’s account is true. Track who makes each claim, then compare it with what the narration later shows. This sequence discusses the ending.
  show: flow Identify the speaker > Distinguish a claim from its reporting > Compare later evidence > Limit the conclusion
demo: “Her great tragedy happened just three years ago,” said the child. The narrator reports this speech, but the account of a tragedy is the child’s claim. Her aunt later names the niece Vera. Quoting Vera does not turn Vera’s claim into an independently confirmed event.
  show: table Layer | What the sentence gives us ;; Vera’s speech | A claim about a tragedy ;; Narrator’s reporting | Attribution of that claim to the child
M: Match each excerpt to the voice that supplies those words. The niece is Vera; the returning men and their dog appear later in the story.
  pairs: Their bodies were never recovered. => Vera | my husband and brothers will be home directly from shooting => Mrs. Sappleton | A tired brown spaniel kept close at their heels. => The narrator
  hint: Distinguish the niece’s account, the aunt’s expectation and the narrator’s description of the returning group.
  why: Vera gives the bog story. Mrs. Sappleton expects the men home. The third-person narrator describes the spaniel following the figures. These are different sources of information within the story.
C: Compare Vera’s account with the later scene. Which conclusion is best supported?
  left: text Vera: “They never came back.”
  right: table Later narration | Excerpt ;; Across the lawn | In the deepening twilight three figures were walking across the lawn towards the window ;; At the window | “Here we are, my dear,” said the bearer of the white mackintosh, coming in through the window
  + The return and conversation undermine Vera’s account that the men never returned :: The later scene gives us more than another assertion by Vera.
  - The narrator confirms that the men are ghosts :: [claim-as-confirmation] Their arrival frightens Framton, but the narrator does not identify the figures as ghosts.
  - Vera herself describes the men returning in this later scene :: [speaker-confusion] The right panel gives narration and the returning man’s speech, not Vera’s account.
  hint: Separate Framton’s frightened interpretation from the return and conversation that the text presents.
  why: The men approach with their dog, enter and speak. Mrs. Sappleton’s expectation fits this ordinary return. Vera’s earlier account of their loss is undermined; Framton’s fear does not independently establish ghosts.
P: After Framton flees, Vera says “he told me he had a horror of dogs”; the narrator ends, “Romance at short notice was her speciality.” How should her dog-phobia explanation be handled?
  + Attribute it to Vera and weigh it against the narrator’s closing comment :: The closing line casts doubt on Vera’s rapid explanations.
  - Report it as independently verified by Framton in this scene :: [reported-claim-as-proof] Vera says that Framton told her; the scene does not independently show him giving her that account.
  - Assume that every narrator’s statement in every story must be false :: [universal-overreach] Evidence about Vera in this story does not establish a rule about all narrators or all stories.
  hint: Who supplies the explanation? What does the final sentence say about that speaker’s storytelling?
  why: Vera supplies a fresh account after the visitor has gone. The narrator’s final comment invites us to doubt that account. A character’s assertion that someone told her something remains a claim to assess, rather than independent confirmation from that person.
>P: Apply the distinction to a fresh sentence from the opening: “Privately he doubted more than ever whether these formal visits on a succession of total strangers would do much towards helping the nerve cure which he was supposed to be undergoing.” Who speaks, and whose mental state is reported?
  + A third-person narrator reports Framton’s private doubt :: Narrative voice and access to a character’s mind are separate questions.
  - Framton tells his own story in the first person :: [voice-access-conflation] Access to Framton’s thought does not make him the narrator; the sentence calls him “he”.
  - The sentence gives only publicly observable action, with no mental state :: [thought-as-action] “Privately he doubted” explicitly reports a mental state.
  hint: Check the pronoun, then identify what “privately he doubted” lets the reader know.
  why: The voice uses the third person while giving access to Framton’s doubt. This sentence supports that local description; it does not establish that the whole story is confined to his perspective.
done: In another story, label who makes a claim and whose thoughts are available. Compare the claim with later evidence before deciding how far it can be trusted.
